course: dictionary
lesson: 620
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The best way to choose the right one is to read the words around it, which is its context. «Babble» is a good example, because it can mean talking quickly, the sounds a baby makes, or the soft sound of running water.
explain: Look at the clues. «She babbled about her holiday for an hour.» Here babbled means talked quickly and hard to follow, because she was talking about a holiday, not making sounds. Test your meaning in the sentence: "She talked quickly about her holiday for an hour." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Now look at «a babbling brook». A brook is a small stream, and water cannot talk, so babbling must mean the soft, bubbling sound the water makes. In each case, the words around the word told you which meaning to choose.
fact: Babble can be a verb (she babbles, they babbled, babbling) or a noun (a babble of voices).
fact: «Babble» can mean talking quickly in a way that is hard to understand, or silly talk.
fact: «Babble» can mean the speech-like sounds a baby makes before real words.
fact: «Babble» can mean the soft, bubbling sound of water, as in «a babbling brook».
fact: «Babies babble ("ba-ba-ba") long before their first words; it is practice with the sounds of language.»
exercise: «She babbled about her holiday for an hour.» What does babbled mean here? | Dropped her bag on the bus | Sang a quiet lullaby | Swam very fast in the sea | *Talked quickly and hard to follow
exercise: «Listen to the babbling brook over the stones.» What is making the babbling sound? | *Water in a stream | A sleepy dog | An old radio | A crowd of shoppers
exercise: Find and copy the word from «Babies babble long before their first words» that names the sounds babies make before real words. | first | *babble | words | babies
test: Ava says, "Babble only ever means one thing." Is she right? | Yes, it only ever means silly talk | Yes, it only ever means a baby's sounds | Yes, it only ever means the sound of water | *No, it has several meanings, and the words around it show which one
test: «Babies who see signing even babble with their hands.» What does babble mean in this sentence? | *Practise language through speech-like movements before real signs | Fall asleep quickly | Argue with a friend | Shout loudly at a party
exam: «The babble of voices in the hall made it hard to hear the teacher.» What does babble mean here? | Sleeping peacefully in a chair | A quiet whisper from a baby | Soft, bubbling water over stones | *A noise of many voices at once
